const eqCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · everything the business owns = assets', note: 'The resources a business controls: cash, inventory, machinery, vehicles, premises (syllabus 1.2). Every one of them had to be paid for somehow — and that "somehow" is the other side of the story.' },
    { math: '2 · each asset was funded two ways', note: 'Either outsiders supplied the funds — liabilities such as bank loans and trade payables — or the owner did. The owner\'s stake in the business is called capital, or owner\'s equity.' },
    { math: '3 · assets = liabilities + capital', note: 'The accounting equation. Not a formula to memorise but a fact to understand: a firm\'s resources must exactly equal the claims on those resources. It balances by construction — always.' },
    { math: '4 · owner invests $10,000 → both sides grow', note: 'Cash (an asset) rises by $10,000 and capital rises by $10,000. One transaction, two effects, equation still equal. This two-sided effect is what the double entry system (syllabus 2) records for every transaction.' },
