<TopicPage slug="subjects/accounting/foundational/the-fundamentals-of-accounting">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the very first lesson in Accounting. You need no prior knowledge at all — no
    business experience, no jargon, and no maths beyond adding, subtracting and a percentage.
    We start from zero and build up one idea at a time. Every new word is explained the
    moment it first appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what book-keeping and accounting each do, and why they are two different jobs;</li>
    <li>give three reasons a business measures its profit or loss, and name who reads the results;</li>
    <li>define asset, liability and capital in plain words;</li>
    <li>state the accounting equation and show, with real transactions, that it never breaks;</li>
    <li>explain the dual effect — the rule that every transaction touches the business in two ways.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph here carries exactly one idea. If one feels heavy, slow
    down — nothing later will rush you.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a fruit stall</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine you run a small fruit stall. On Monday you pay 20 dollars for boxes of oranges.
    During the day you sell all of them for 32 dollars. In the evening two questions are on
    your mind: how much did I take, and did I actually make money? Memory will not answer
    that honestly, so you write it down. That notebook — every sale, every purchase, every
    payment — is where accounting begins.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each thing you write down is a <strong>transaction</strong>: any event where money or
    goods move between the business and the outside world. Recording every transaction
    accurately and in order is called <strong>book-keeping</strong>. Everything built on top
    of that record — checking it, summarising it, explaining it to others — is
    <strong>accounting</strong>. The subject contains both jobs, in that order: record
    first, then interpret.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your stall hides one more idea worth noticing. There are two different stories to tell
    about it. One is a <em>photograph</em>: what you have and what you owe right now — the
    till, the unsold fruit, the 5 dollars you borrowed from your neighbour. The other is a
    <em>video</em>: what happened over the week — what you sold, what you spent, what was
    left over. This lesson builds both stories: the video becomes the profit calculation,
    and the photograph becomes the accounting equation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a business records every transaction (book-keeping), then
    checks, sums up and explains those records (accounting). Two views matter — a photograph
    of what the business has and owes, and a video of what it did.
  </p>

  <h2>Book-keeping versus accounting, drawn precisely</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Book-keeping</strong> is the detailed recording of financial transactions:
    writing up the ledger accounts from invoices, receipts and till rolls. It is clerical
    and rule-following. A book-keeper records that 500 dollars left the till on Tuesday —
    and that is all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Accounting</strong> uses those recorded results in three ways. First it checks
    and verifies them: do the records agree with the bank, with each other, with reality?
    Second it summarises them into financial statements — the photograph becomes the
    statement of financial position, and the video becomes the statement of profit or loss.
    Third it interprets and communicates the results to the people who need them for
    decisions. An accountant can tell you whether that missing 500 dollars was an expense
    (fuel), an asset (part-payment of a machine) or evidence of theft. Three stories, one
    book-keeping fact — only interpretation tells them apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    The split explains who does what. Book-keeping is routine, and modern software does much
    of it — the till, the bank feed, the invoices. Accounting cannot be reduced the same way,
    because its essence is judgement: which figures belong in the statements, how to value an
    asset, whether a debt will really be paid. That is also why accounting has a profession.
    Anyone can follow a recording rule. But standing behind a summary and saying "this is
    true" takes trained, accountable people.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> book-keeping and accounting are the same job at two skill
  levels, the way "junior" and "senior" are. They are different activities. Book-keeping
  records what happened; accounting checks, summarises and interprets the record. There is no
  accounting without book-keeping — but a warehouse of perfectly recorded evidence that
  nobody has interpreted is still not accounting.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> book-keeping records; accounting verifies, summarises and
    communicates. Next: why anyone bothers to measure profit in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Why measure profit and loss at all</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Revenue</strong> is the money a business earns from sales.
    <strong>Expenses</strong> are the costs of earning it. <strong>Profit</strong> is what is
    left when you subtract expenses from revenue. A <strong>loss</strong> is the same sum
    running the other way — expenses bigger than revenue. Three purposes make this one
    subtraction worth an entire subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, <strong>to show whether trading is working</strong> — whether the business is
    adding to the owner's wealth or quietly draining it. A shop can look busy, pay its bills
    and still be shrinking its owner's wealth by 200 dollars a week. Only the profit
    calculation reveals that.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, <strong>to provide information for monitoring progress</strong>. This year's
    profit compared with last year's, with the plan, with a competitor's margin. A falling
    margin is invisible on any single day and obvious in a comparison. Monitoring is what
    turns a number into a control.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, <strong>to support decision-making</strong>: expand, borrow, change prices, close
    a product line. These are judgements, and they rest on accounting numbers — and not only
    for owners. <strong>Lenders</strong> read the accounts before advancing loans.
    <strong>Managers</strong> use them to set targets. <strong>Governments</strong> tax
    profits. Potential <strong>investors and buyers</strong> price the business from them.
    Profit is the scoreboard that lets a business be managed rather than merely run.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice what those uses demand. The numbers must be consistent, so that this year can be
    compared with last year. They must be truthful rather than flattering, so that decisions
    rest on reality. The later rules of accounting — principles, verification, standards —
    all grow from these two demands.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> profit (revenue minus expenses) is measured to show whether
    trading works, to monitor progress, and to support decisions — for owners, lenders,
    managers, governments and investors. Now we build the equation that ties everything
    together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the accounting equation, built up step by step from what a business owns and who funded it" widget="equationStep" config={eqCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the six steps in order. Every step moves
    the two sides together — an owner investment grows assets and capital by the same
    amount, a van bought on credit grows assets and liabilities by the same amount. By the
    final step the equation has survived profit itself, which is the point: it balances by
    construction, not by careful book-keeping.
  </p>

  <h2>The accounting equation: the fact that never breaks</h2>
  <p>
    Three master words carry the whole subject. An <strong>asset</strong> is a resource of
    value that the business owns or controls — cash, inventory (goods held for sale),
    machinery, vehicles, premises, and the amounts customers owe <em>to</em> the firm. A
    <strong>liability</strong> is an amount the business owes to outsiders — bank loans,
    unpaid supplier bills, unpaid wages. <strong>Capital</strong>, also called the owner's
    equity, is the owner's stake: what the business would owe back to the owner if
    everything were sold and every debt paid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the <strong>accounting equation</strong>:
  </p>
  <p><code>assets = liabilities + capital</code></p>
  <p>
    Read it as a funding story, not a formula. Every asset the business holds had to be paid
    for somehow. Only two "somehows" exist: outsiders supplied the money (liabilities), or
    the owner did (capital). So a firm's resources must exactly equal the claims on those
    resources — not because accountants are tidy, but because it could not be otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch the equation survive a real first day. Amina starts a courier business and invests
    10,000 dollars of her own money. Cash (an asset) rises by 10,000 dollars, and capital
    rises by 10,000 dollars. Both sides grew equally, so the equation holds. Next she buys a
    van for 5,000 dollars using a bank loan. The van (an asset) rises by 5,000 dollars and
    the loan (a liability) rises by 5,000 dollars — this time an outsider funded the asset,
    and the equation holds again. She now has assets of 15,000 dollars, liabilities of
    5,000 dollars and capital of 10,000 dollars. Slide the equation around and it hands you
    a useful definition: <code>capital = assets − liabilities</code>. Accountants call that
    number the owner's <em>net assets</em> — it is the number that profit exists to grow.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first widget above builds this same story in six steps. Click through them until the
    funding logic feels obvious rather than memorised.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="drive the equation yourself — assets and liabilities set equity; revenue and expenses set profit" widget="sliderLab" config={acctLab} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the assets slider and the owner's equity readout
    follows it one-for-one; drag liabilities upward and equity falls step for step. No
    combination of sliders ever breaks the first readout — that is the accounting equation
    acting as a constraint rather than a coincidence. Then set revenue and expenses and
    watch the other two readouts answer the video side's question: did trading add to the
    owner's stake?
  </p>

  <p>
    The lab lets you drive the equation yourself. The first readout is the equation turned
    around: whatever you do to the sliders, the owner's equity stays pinned at assets minus
    liabilities. It is a constraint, not a coincidence. The other readouts compute the video
    side: profit is revenue minus expenses, and the <strong>profit margin</strong> expresses
    that profit as a percentage of sales. With the default sliders, profit is 50,000 minus
    37,000, which gives 13,000 dollars, and the margin is 26 per cent. Is that good? There is
    no universal answer. Grocery chains run single-digit margins on enormous turnover;
    software firms run huge margins on modest capital. A margin only means something against
    a benchmark — which is the "monitoring progress" purpose from earlier, come back around.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> assets are the resources; liabilities and capital are the two
    parties who funded them; the equation says the two must match, transaction after
    transaction. The mechanism behind that match has a name — the dual effect.
  </p>

  <h2>The dual effect: why every transaction has two sides</h2>
  <p>
    Every transaction affects the business in at least two ways. This is the
    <strong>dual effect</strong>, and it is why the equation survives everything. Sell goods
    for 800 dollars in cash that cost you 500 dollars: inventory falls by 500, cash rises by
    800, and the 300-dollar difference is profit — which belongs to the owner, so capital
    rises by 300. Pay a 900-dollar electricity bill: cash falls by 900, and the expense cuts
    profit, so capital falls by 900 too. Take out a loan: cash and liabilities both rise.
    Repay it: both fall. The owner withdraws 250 dollars for personal use: cash falls, and
    capital falls too. That withdrawal is called <strong>drawings</strong> — it is not an
    expense, because the owner is not "costing" the firm; withdrawals simply come out of the
    owner's stake.
  </p>
  <p>
    In every case, the two sides stay equal. The <strong>double entry system</strong> is the
    recording machine built on this fact: each transaction is entered twice, once as a debit
    and once as a credit, so the books cannot drift out of balance without the drift showing
    up in a failed trial balance. The double entry system has its own lesson later in this
    course.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> profit and cash are the same thing. They are not, and the
  difference is the first lesson accountants learn the hard way. A profitable business can run
  out of cash — it has made sales on credit (customers have not paid yet), or it spent cash on
  inventory and machines that have not turned back into cash yet. A loss-making business can be
  flush with cash — the owner just injected capital, or it is selling off its assets. Profit
  measures performance <em>over a period</em>; cash measures a balance <em>at a moment</em>. The
  two readouts in the lab above are deliberately independent for exactly this reason.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A business buys a delivery van with a bank loan. What happens to each side of the accounting equation?</li>
    <li>The owner of a shop takes 100 dollars from the till for a birthday present. Is that an expense?</li>
    <li>Why can a profitable business still run out of cash?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Both sides rise by the same amount — the van is a new
    asset, the loan is a new liability, and the equation still balances. (2) No — it is
    drawings, a withdrawal from capital; expenses are costs of running the business, not
    personal takings. (3) Because profit is measured over a period while cash is a balance
    at a moment: sales made on credit and cash spent on stock or machines mean the profit
    has not turned back into cash yet.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> if a business's assets grow, its owner must be getting
  richer. Not necessarily — growth funded by borrowing leaves capital untouched, because each
  new asset is exactly matched by a new liability. Only funding by the owner or by profit
  grows the owner's stake.</p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Book-keeping records every transaction; accounting checks, summarises and interprets the record.</li>
    <li>Profit (revenue minus expenses) is measured to show whether trading works, to monitor progress and to support decisions.</li>
    <li>An asset is a resource the business controls; a liability is an outsider's claim on it; capital is the owner's stake.</li>
    <li>The accounting equation — assets equal liabilities plus capital — balances by construction, transaction after transaction.</li>
    <li>Every transaction touches the business in at least two ways: the dual effect.</li>
    <li>Profit is measured over a period; cash is a balance at a moment — a profitable business can still run out of cash.</li>
    <li>Drawings are a withdrawal from the owner's stake, not an expense of the business.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson builds the recording machine this equation demands: the double entry
    system, where every transaction is entered twice — once as a debit and once as a credit —
    so the books cannot drift out of balance without the drift showing in a failed trial
    balance. Later lessons refine how profit is timed and how the two stories, photograph and
    video, are drawn up as full financial statements.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two refinements show how the simple equation grows into professional accounting. The
    first is the <em>timing</em> of profit. Our courier example assumed revenue and expenses
    hit the period cleanly, but real businesses earn revenue before or after they are paid,
    and they use up benefits continuously — insurance paid in advance, vans wearing out.
    Accounting therefore measures profit on the <em>accruals</em> basis: match revenues and
    expenses to the period in which they are earned or consumed, not to when the cash moves.
    That is why a van bought for 12,000 dollars and lasting six years reduces profit by about
    2,000 dollars a year (depreciation), not by 12,000 dollars in year one. The equation is
    unchanged; only the care in defining "expense" has grown.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second refinement is the equation's family of forms. A <em>working-capital</em>
    version separates short-term items (<code>assets − current liabilities = non-current
    assets + working capital</code>) to study liquidity. Analysts also rearrange the
    equation into <code>capital = assets − liabilities</code> and track that number across
    the years — because the change in equity over a period, after allowing for what the
    owner put in and took out, is the profit. That is a backwards check: the "video" and the
    "photograph" must agree with each other. When they do not, someone has made an error —
    or told a lie — and tracing which is the auditor's ancient trade.
  </p>
